    "faq_hot_honey": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_spice": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make the hot honey glaze milder — or hotter?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, and it is the easiest change in the recipe. Drop the sriracha to <strong>2 teaspoons</strong> and leave the jalapeño out of the salsa for a kid-friendly version that still tastes like hot honey. For real heat, push it to <strong>3 tablespoons</strong> plus a pinch of chili flake and leave the jalapeño seeds in. The Dijon and honey stay at 2 tablespoons either way — see the heat chart above.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_temp": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What internal temperature should the salmon reach?",
            "text": "<p>Bake at 400°F for <strong>10–12 minutes</strong> and pull it at around <strong>130°F</strong> at the thickest point. Carryover heat brings it to roughly 145–150°F while it rests for 3–5 minutes, which gives you a moist, flaky fillet instead of a dry one. Read our full <a href=\"/pages/the-ultimate-guide-to-salmon-internal-temp\" title=\"The Ultimate Guide to Salmon Internal Temp\">guide to salmon internal temp</a>.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_marinate": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do I really have to marinate for 30 minutes?",
            "text": "<p>Thirty minutes is the sweet spot — long enough for the Dijon to grip the fillet so the glaze does not slide off in the oven. In a rush, 10 minutes still works. Going much past an hour is not useful here: unlike a miso marinade, the acid in the mustard starts to firm up the surface of the fish.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_mango": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My mango is not ripe. What now?",
            "text": "<p>Leave it in a paper bag on the counter for a day or two — it should give slightly when you press near the stem. If you need salsa tonight, thawed frozen mango chunks work well; drain them and pat dry so the salsa does not go watery. If the mango is still a little tart, a ½ teaspoon of honey in the salsa closes the gap.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How far ahead can I make each component?",
            "text": "<p>Coconut rice holds <strong>4 days</strong> refrigerated. The glaze keeps <strong>1 week</strong> whisked and sealed. Salsa is best on <strong>days 1–2</strong> — after that the mango softens and the cucumber weeps. Cooked salmon keeps <strong>3 days</strong> and is delicious cold, so assembled bowls make excellent lunches with no reheating at all.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_defrost": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I defrost Seatopia salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Defrost in the refrigerator overnight, wrapped in paper, to preserve texture and nutrient integrity. For a faster option, drop the vacuum-sealed portion into a bowl of cold water for 30–60 minutes. Never thaw at room temperature or in the microwave.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_spice",
